EU: Not yet. Not yet. And I would like to tell about at this point that my grandmother, Yocheved Kafeman, and my grandfather, Eliyahu Kafeman and their two sons-- my mother's brothers-- left Poland around 1930-something-- early.

My mother's brothers were ardent Zionists-- Eleizer, the older brother, and Michael, the younger brother. And they left for Palestine. They underwent a certain training before they could go. I recall having been told about that. And both my uncles and my Kafeman grandparents left for Palestine. It wasn't until 1939 that my Grandmother Yocheved came for a visit to us in Lódz.

INT: Before we go into that, are there other extended relatives who were important to your life that you'd like to mention?

EU: Yes, yes. I remember in particular one aunt and uncle. They were really my parents' aunt and uncle. Their last name was Vole-- Uncle Henrick Vole and Aunt Andia Vole, and their daughter, who her name was Nina, and she was a pharmacist.

