INT: Before we go into that, are there other relatives still you'd like to talk about-- any other aunts and uncles?

EU: Not that I remember any-- any particular other relatives. I could-- I don't-- I remember some talk of some, but not from my own memory.

INT: Tell me about your family's religious practices.

EU: What I remember as a little girl is that my parents were looked down on what they considered outmoded Orthodox practices. I don't remember much of a traditional Jewish life when I was a little girl. Now, I don't know if it didn't exist or if I simply don't remember.

My mother came from a traditional family. Her parents kept kosher. We did not. I do know that my parents considered themselves religious in their own interpretation of their religion, but there weren't too many-- too many things that I could point to that would-- that I could remember as far as any religious practices that-- those days.

INT: Do you remember attending a synagogue?

EU: I don't remember.

INT: Do you remember holidays-- Jewish holidays?

EU: I really don't. I don't remember.

INT: So what did-- what did being Jewish mean to you at that time?

EU: Well, this is when I was five and six or four. And I didn't have a concept of it. I knew I was Jewish, but I didn't have a concept of what that meant at all in those days.

INT: Were you at all aware of antisemitism then?

EU: No, no I wasn't-- not that early on.

INT: Did you continue to live in your Grandmother Wolman's large apartment?

EU: Well, as a matter of fact, no we did not. I don't remember when, but when my father's business wasn't doing so well, they were forced to give that up and move into a smaller apartment, over which my Grandmother Wolman was very unhappy.

INT: Did she move with you?

EU: She moved with us, and I remember a bit more about that place. Again, I don't know quite how old I was, but I had to be, I am guessing, five, six years old.

I do remember in particular my parents' bedroom, and a-- my mother's dresser with a lovely round mirror over it. I think I just enjoyed playing with things that were on the dresser. I even have even little details, like a powder box that she kept. It was-- it had little orange umbrellas on it, and some other pretty bottles and things that were on that dresser. And I remember looking at them, admiring them-- playing with them.

INT: Do you remember the address or street of that apartment?

EU: I think it was on Bednarska, but I don't really recall the exact number. Again, it must have-- it was probably first or second floor. I remember going upstairs.

INT: Did your nanny stay with you there also?

EU: Yes. Yes, she stayed with us. And again, we would play. She would take me to different-- to the park in the neighborhood. I'm saying different ones, but I think it was pretty much in the neighborhood. And there were some other children on the playground, but I don't recall any names or any particular children that I could speak of.

INT: How did your family spend your summer months?

EU: That's one of my fondest memories of my childhood, because each year we would go by train to a little village outside Lódz called Zakowice. It couldn't have been far from there.

But we spent several months-- most of the summer months. Those were-- there was a group of cottages, and my-- several relatives and friends of my parents were there each summer together. It was a carefree, happy time.

I remember beautiful forests, particularly white birches, and fields of sunflowers. And I would pick the-- pick the seeds and eat the seeds from the sunflowers.

I remember going with my grandmother and my mother picking berries-- some kind of-- something that was growing very low, because we had to bend down in-- in-- around the forest. They were beautiful green meadows.

And I remember in the early evening walking, running, jumping with my mother towards the train station when my father would come home in the evening. That's how I know this Zakowice. Couldn't have been far from Lódz, because my father would come home in the evening.

I remember the evenings when the grown-ups would sit on the veranda of this cottage and play cards by kerosene light. And it was such a happy, carefree time. And I-- who could have foreseen on those special days and evenings what was about to happen to my family?

INT: Let's now talk about the summer of 1939. How was that summer different than the previous ones for you?

EU: Well, it started out like all the others. And what I just described was exactly what I remember of that last summer. But unbeknown to us, there was war brewing, which-- I'm ahead of myself. We didn't know it, of course, then.

But at some point, the word-- my parents must have heard or found out that indeed there was something happening in-- on way of-- of a war or some confrontation between Poland and Germany. And that it was decided that we needed to get back home to Lódz.

And we left on one-- we left in a hurry. Things were packed quickly, and we took the train back home to Lódz.

My Grandmother Kafeman was with us during that summer in Zakowice. I remember that she was with us. She came to visit us from what was then Palestine [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] in 1939.

INT: And what happened when you returned to Lódz?

EU: From then on, I would have to chronologically tell you just how things happen. I remember getting back home, and the first thing that I remember was that my father, along with other men, was told to leave to go towards Lódz to help the Polish army, because Germany was about to occupy Poland.

And I remember there was whispering going on. I wasn't quite seven years old, and, of course, didn't quite know what was really going on. But things were changing, and I do remember when my father was living how upset we were.

But he and other Polish men-- Jews and non-Jews alike-- were told to head towards Warsaw. And I remember saying goodbye to him and-- when-- when he was leaving, only to return very shortly afterwards. And then I recall what he was telling the family that-- as they were-- there were lots and lots of people on the highways going. And they were being bombed. There was-- there were bombings all around, and they never made it quite to Warsaw. But instead, they went back home.

And I remember him coming home to us, telling us all this. And, of course, I didn't know the significance. No one really knew what was about to happen at that point.

INT: Let's stop now and change the tape.INT: --father returning to Lódz, after he went off to hopefully fight the-- the Germans that were invading the country. It was a happy return. You were glad to see him. Had you started school, at that time?

EU: No. As a matter of fact, I remember looking forward to it so much. I was about to start first grade. And I remember my birthday gift that for some reason, that year, was so important and so significant to me. Because it was this beautiful maroon leather-bound case.

Somehow I was supposed to take that to first grade with me, but those-- that was the custom of those days. And it had gold embossed, my name spelled out, Krystyna, on it. I was very much looking forward to first grade. And I never-- it never happened.

INT: How did you feel about that?

EU: I was sad. I also-- well, I should start with the first experiences that I remember of occupation. Poland was occupied rather quickly. Lódz, suddenly, I remember there were German soldiers in Lódz.

And it was common practice for them to come into people's homes, Jewish homes, and take anything they wanted to. And I remember that. Because it was such a scary thing, to have these strangers, in their uniforms, come and take things that belonged to us.

Also, it became dangerous for me to go outside to play, not only because of the German soldiers, but there were young Polish hooligans out in the street who harassed Jews, humiliated people, beat people up. It wasn't safe for me to go outside. And it wasn't safe for my parents to go outside. To get-- to go to the grocery store was dangerous. My father could no longer go to work.

I remember one incident when he went, went to town and came home very upset. And he-- some German, some German soldiers took him and other men, and made them carry a large piano from someone's home that they have taken. And my dad was bruised. And I remember that was terrible, when he came home and told us about that.

I-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] one incident that I remember clearly was that of these-- of the German soldiers coming to our house, both my grandmothers, my mother, father, and I, were there. And I remember them coming in. And they were in the entry, in the hallway.

And they demanded [NON-ENGLISH]. They wanted [NON-ENGLISH], which are suitcases. And my mother stood up and announced that she didn't have any. 'We don't have any [NON-ENGLISH].'

And-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] they then, they said, well, they're going to search for it. And we all, I even, knew that we did. Because we just unpacked those, that we brought back from our vacation in Zakowice. Well, they proceeded and indeed, found leather suitcases and brought it out and said, 'Well, what is that?'

And mother, without blinking an eye, said, '[NON-ENGLISH].' that was just another name for suitcases. She knew very well that [NON-ENGLISH] and Koffer was one in the same.

But in that instance, she just had the presence of mind to-- to say that. And my grandmothers and my father were absolutely terrified. They just thought that mother would be shot on the spot. But the man just, they just took their loot and left.

Another time they came and took the chandelier that was over the dining room table. Those were very scary moments. The word got around, when they were coming from one neighbor to the other.

And I also remember one German soldier, in particular. He came with a group of soldiers and took different things and noticed pretty felt appliqué pillowcases on our sofa and took those, all of them. This same man returned later and said that he wants more of those. Where did my parents get those? I even remember his name. His name was Deiberlem.

And mother said, well, she made those. And she could make some for him. But in exchange, she would need some flour and some milk and some sugar.

And again, to the amazement of my grandmothers and my dad, the man said, OK. He will bring those. And indeed, he did. And Mother stayed up late at night, sewing, making pillowcases. And we had a little more food.

Food was becoming scarce. If it were not for Furmankowa, bringing us some, it would be we, we would really have a difficult time not having enough.

INT: What about regulations imposed against the Jews?

EU: Yes. At-- I don't know at what time, but sometime around then, we were ordered to wear the yellow stars with the word 'Jude,' printed across. We had to wear those on our-- anytime we were outside-- outer garments, the front and the back so we could be easily recognized by anyone and humiliated, harassed, beaten up, mistreated in any form that people felt it was permissible. And anything was permissible, against Jews. So I remember Mother sewing those on.

I don't remember any particular feelings about that. As a matter of fact, I-- I remember events. But I don't remember how I felt, at that time. I wish I did.

I remember being frightened, but at the same time, safe. I was a little girl. I was seven years old. And I was with my parents. What could possibly happen to me?

INT: What about other regulations? Were you aware of any?

EU: Not any particular ones, other than-- no, I'm really not. I wasn't aware of any.

Now that you mention, and now once in a while, when it comes to that sort of thing, I don't recall if I remember it from then or if I was told, that the Jews were not allowed to walk on the sidewalks, that they have to step to the side when Germans or Poles were going. It was a deliberate attempt to reduce us and our self awareness. But I wasn't, I didn't understand any of it then.

INT: Did your family consider trying to-- to flee to other areas that were not occupied by the Nazis? Or perhaps going into hiding?

EU: As far as I know, not. There was no place to go. Of course, I think their thoughts were also, they've gone through difficult times, outbursts of antisemitism in Poland, before. And I just remember thinking that all this will pass and things will go back to normal. There was no doubt, in my child's mind, that everything would be all right again.

So now and also, my Grandmother Wolman was ill, at that time. There was no place to go. No one-- no one wanted us, for that matter. We had no recourse but just to go on from one day to the next, of course, not realizing what was about to happen. Maybe, in hindsight, maybe if people had known they would have done whatever. Impossible, seemed at that time, to leave. But no, there was no talk about it, as far as I know.

INT: How long did you continue to stay in your apartment home?

EU: I don't know. But I do remember that eventually, the order was given that we would be relocated. I didn't quite know what that meant. But different people were relocated at different times.

And when it came our turn, my parents and some other friends or neighbors, in the evening, buried some of their valuables, family heirlooms, silver, and so on, in the courtyard of this apartment, thinking that they will come back and claim their belongings. It was done at night. I remember that.

And then, the day came when we were made to leave our homes. And I was assured that everything would be all right. It was winter. It was cold. I now know it was winter of 1940. And I do remember that day.

How we-- mother kept putting clothing on me, as much as I could possibly wear. Because we were only allowed to take which they could carry. So I had dresses and blouses and sweaters. And I remember feeling rather stiff with all this on.

And I remember my parents putting things in these large European-sized pillowcases. Because they were lighter, making bundles of blankets and pots and pans and everything that they could possibly carry. And I remember leaving home, holding on to my favorite doll, a rag doll.

[CRYING] Her name was [? Bodwiga. ?] Leaving all the other pretty dolls and their beautiful festive clothes behind, I had a choice and I decided on that doll. I was going to come back to all my other toys. I remember that lovely tea seat that I got for this last birthday at home.

All this stayed behind, as we left our home, joining others. Group of people, I remember lots of people, all walking together, marching with-- with soldier, German soldiers, guards, alongside. I didn't know where we were going. I had no idea. And it-- it was very, very cold, terribly cold. I remember my face, just freezing.

And if-- I don't know how long we walked. But eventually, we arrived at the place which was then to be known as the Lodz Lódz ghetto. It was in a place called Baluty, which was an old, dilapidated, rundown part of Lódz.

We were assigned into a room in an old apartment building. My parents, grandmothers, several other relatives, and other people. It was crowded. It was dingy. It was dirty. It didn't have any bathroom facilities that we were used to, or heat, I remember. Again, this was upstairs. I remember the hallway, going up there.

And it was my Grandmother Wolman who had such a hard time with it. I think my parents put on a brave face for me. And of course, Mother immediately, immediately set out to make the best of this situation.

There was some kind of an old sofa, some piece of furniture. And she pulled out her clean sheets and blanket and made a bed for me there. And I remember lying down there, the first night. Didn't even take all of the clothes off, and just pulling the blanket feather bed over my body and spending that first night in that place, which was to be home for me, for a number of years.

My, as I mentioned, Grandma Wolman was ill, not feeling well. Grandmother Kafeman was doing her best to assist with everything. And now we had-- my parents had to register in order to be given some food rations. And after a while, the only way people would have food, which from now on in my story is the most important thing. It was the lifeline, food. In order to be-- to get food, one had to have a job.

I must mention that the Lódz ghetto, which I found out after the war, was different from others. Because they were, the Germans set up factories in the ghetto which were to serve their war effort. At the very start of our stay there, Furmankowa did manage to bring some food to us and I think even some of our belongings, before this ghetto was totally sealed off.

And eventually it was completely sealed off. No one could leave. No one could enter. It was a prison. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

I remember my parents searching for work, which was absolutely essential. Because of the factories, the people who could find jobs the easiest were those in the trades, tailors, metal workers, carpenters. For business people like my-- my father, mother, there was really no work. And it was getting to be a very difficult situation. Mother did get a job, eventually, in a soup kitchen, which was so important to us. Because that meant little more food.

My father, after for a long time searching for some kind of work, met an acquaintance who was, at that time, with the Jewish ghetto administration, called Judenrate. And after a long while, which seemed hopeless, my father was so happy to have found a position with the Judenrate, which meant that we would have some food to eat.

At that time, there was also some talk that those men who didn't have work would be separated from their families and sent somewhere else, some other camp. Those were a rumor. I don't know if it was true or not. Perhaps it was. But here we were. [SIGHS] I--

INT: What-- what were your father's responsibilities in his job?

EU: At first, minimal. He was assigned to patrol the streets, to see that things were run as smoothly as they were supposed to have been. What I think is important at this point, for me, is to tell you that there were-- there was an attempt at a school. People in the ghetto tried to have some dignity, to go on with some normal form of life.

There were schools set up. There was a hospital. There were even some concerts. People wrote, composed music. I attended that school. I don't remember a great deal. It must not have lasted long.

But with time, my Grandmother Wolman died in the ghetto. She was very ill. And she died with her family surrounding her. I remember the day. I remembered the other people coming to our apartment and milling about. And I wasn't allowed to come in the room.

And I remember being sad that Grandma Wolman had died. I think that there was a funeral held, as was still possible in those days. I don't remember if I attended it or not.

As time went on, I just remember being in this room, in which we stayed, by the way, all the time we were in the ghetto. I have since found out that it was, the name of the street was, Franciszkanska, somewhere in that-- in that street is where we lived all the time. We were there.

INT: When-- when you stopped attending the school or when the school ended, did you continue your studies in any way?

EU: Well, I-- there was someone who came to us. And I took some lessons from this young man in just general subject, also, some religion and some Hebrew. I actually learned some Hebrew prayers at that time. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

My-- you ask about my father's responsibilities. As time went on, he was involved in, with other men, in guarding the food supply that was-- that came into the ghetto, seeing to it that it was-- that the distribution points had the food so that everyone could get their share. There were-- as the situation worsened in the ghetto, there was such incredible hunger that people were starving in the streets.

And many young, strong people would try and rob the food supplies, as it was brought in from the bakeries and so on, which is understandable. But it was my father's duty to see to it that the food supply did reach its destination. And it was quite a responsibility.

I do remember him being-- talking about it now and then. I also remember one incident, in particular, that I probably overheard. Because I was still very protected and sheltered by my parents, from knowing what was happening. But I remember my father coming home once, terribly upset. Some German soldiers made him go with him, through the streets of the ghetto, and bragging, what a good sharpshooter he was, and pointing to a woman in an upstairs window, and telling my father to watch how he would aim for her eye. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

So I overheard that. Because I was very sheltered and protected. I didn't really know what was going on. Because of my father's position and mother's, I was incredibly fortunate. I did not starve.

INT: How did that incident affect your father?

EU: [SOBBING] It was very difficult, very difficult. And it was just whispered. I was still their little girl, and I didn't really know.

It affected him enormously. But there was really no choice but to go on. When people often speak about resistance, our resistance, from then on, through horrendous times to come, was to just make it through this day and the next under any circumstances. And this is how I was always told everything will be all right again. Everything will be all right.

I do remember, in those days, I always enjoyed the drawing and painting. I don't think I had any paint, at that point. But I remember drawing a lot. I wish I had some of those things.

INT: Do you remember any certain aktions or roundups--

EU: Yes. Yes.

INT: --that occurred in the ghetto?

EU: As time went on, people were brought into the ghetto, strangers, lots of people, from everywhere. I remember hearing different languages, Hungary, Hungarian, particularly. And also, people were taken away. And I didn't know then what the word aktion meant.

But they were taken away to other places. As the situation worsened, some people thought, well it couldn't be much better. And they willingly went on these early transports, to what they were promised would be better places, especially for their children. They would be taken where there would be more food, fresh air, and so on.

I must, speaking of children, I remember one girl. I had one friend. I don't remember much about her, but I do remember what she looked like. She had pretty red hair and her name was Basha. I don't know her last name, but I remember being with her, sometime.

INT: Would you go to each other's room or home?

EU: Yes. My memories are very distant. But I do remember being with her, somewhere, talking with her, playing, in a way. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

The aktionen continued. And it became sort of routine almost, that the Nazis would come through and pick up children. After a while, I think the adults realized that maybe there was more to it than just taking the children to better conditions.

And I remember one event, when there was an old, dried-out water well, in the courtyard of this building. And when word reached the grown ups, my parents-- my mother, I think whoever was home with me, well, both my parents included, that they were coming. And I remember there was like, sirens blaring. The adults put a ladder into this dried out water well in the courtyard.

Myself and some other children were led down there. And the lid was put on top. And we stayed there, in the dark. I often been thinking in recent years, how I felt? What did I think? I don't remember.

INT: Do you know how long you stayed there?

EU: I don't remember. My time references are so scarce anyway. I remember being there.

And then the lid was lifted. And I was, and the other children, came out. That is pretty much all that I really remember of those years. And I wish I remembered more. I have been trying so hard, but I don't have any-- any detailed memories.

As the time went on, people were leaving more, all the time. And the order was given for us eventually to leave also. We were to be resettled.

Because of my father's connections, we stayed in the ghetto and left with the last or second-to-the-last transport, with the metalworkers. Because they were seen as skilled metalworkers and were supposed to have been sent to a factory somewhere in Germany, to-- to work for the Germans. Of course, anytime one worked for someone, that meant a little more food. We saw everything through how much food was available.

INT: Do-- when was that, that you left the ghetto?

EU: I believe it was in August of 1944.

INT: And had you ever done any metalwork? Or had your mother or father?

EU: I haven't. I don't think that they did either. I-- I doubt it. But they were to leave with the metalworkers' transport.

INT: And what do you remember about that transport?

EU: Oh, I do remember that well. I remember, again, wearing what we had, taking along what was still there. And there wasn't much. And I wasn't wearing quite as much as on my original relocation.

I do remember when the order was given for us to assemble, on a certain day, certain time. And we were being led to the train station. I remember going. I think I was still hugging [? Bodwiga. ?] [SOBBING] She must have been just shreds at that time, but I loved her.

I don't remember carrying anything else. But my parents did. And again, I don't know what I felt. I was with my parents. I was safe.

Us and other people reached the train station. And we were-- there were these, this train, cattle cars, wooden cattle cars. And the step up, we were told to go up. And the step was quite high. I remember I couldn't reach up, my father lifting me, putting me up. My parents and other people that were still-- my Grandmother Kafeman-- I don't remember if they were any other relatives, at this point.

INT: I'm sorry, we're going to have to stop now and change the tape.INT: About your arrival at a camp after the transport.

EU: Let me tell you about the departure first. We were, as I was saying, taken, put into this train. And eventually, more and more people were also put into this same cattle car. And I remember how, with time, there was no room to sit. We were so tightly packed in there. I was just pushed further back as more people were brought on. And it was getting very warm. It was-- the condition was horrendous.

I was standing there, kind of small with adults around me. My grandmother was getting ill. I don't know how long we stood there. Eventually, the door was closed, and I remember that loud noise of the metal-- metal against metal, closing of the door. And we were just packed as tight as possible in there. And we just stood. I don't know how long.

People were getting ill. Some were crying. Some were moaning. Some were just sliding down to the floor. Many were weak to start out with. There was no water. There was no air. There was no bathroom facility. At one point, they-- while we're still on the ground, they opened the door, and passed bucket around. And then door closed again. And we still stood there.

And eventually, the train started moving. I don't remember how long. But I remember the sound of that steam engine whistle. I think it was nighttime. There were no windows, nothing. We were just-- and it moved, and it picked up speed. And I remember my mother's hand on my shoulder, kind of assuring me. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

And we just kept moving. And eventually, the train stopped. We just stood there. And this horrible smell started penetrating the car, horrible smell. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] And there was someone on there, some men who had a pocketknife, and was making-- cutting into the wood of the-- of the car, to see where we were. And he said something.

And I remember my father saying to my mother, something. They were whispering. And she got upset with him, because he said he's heard rumors of some places. And indeed, if they were true, and we were at such a place, if he had known, we would have all taken poison. And Mother was terribly angry, saying don't ever say such a thing. And we just stood there.

And then suddenly, suddenly, the door was opened, with a loud noise again. And from that moment on, whatever I went through before, it's just dwarfed by comparison. We were ordered to jump out. Out, out. Schnell! Out of this train, raus! Schnell! We were pushed, shoved.

Men and women were immediately separated. I just suddenly didn't see my father anymore. It was just chaos, lots and lots of people, people crying, shouting, babies crying, children. I didn't know what was happening. But at that time, children were taken away from their mothers. And my mother's hold on me never let go.

I remember the red sky, and that awful stench, which I didn't know what it was then. But I learned since. It was the smell of burning human skin. We didn't know where we were. But we were in Auschwitz.

As we got off the train, there were these soldiers, SS I believe. To me they were all uniforms, the same. But I remember their starched uniforms and shiny black boots, and these huge German Shepherds at their side. And they were whipping people, beating them, and forcing everyone to go a certain way.

And-- and I stayed with my mother. We kept being made to run along this platform. And on the side, I remember on the right side, there was like a mountain of stuff-- suitcases, all kinds of things. And I never-- I didn't see any of my things. Some-- it was all torn away, whatever we had.

And we made, we were made to run quickly towards this one area. And when we arrived there, we were taken into this large hall, large building, at which women were sitting at these long tables. And they were processing us. First, we had to take all our clothes off.

And then I remember one woman grabbing my hair. I had long braids, cutting off, and the other one shaving my head. The same thing to my mother's head, my-- my grandmother was-- we were still together, and all the other people. And everyone was in a state of shock, total shock, didn't know what was happening.

From there, we were made to run into another adjacent big room. And there were shower heads overhead. And unbeknown to us, we were so lucky. Because out of these shower-heads, indeed, water came down on us. I remember standing there cold, nude, wet.

And my mother had some sort of a strange reaction, because she just grabbed me, and she started laughing. It was a nervous laughter of some sort. And she hugged me and she said, I kept feeling my head, and she says, hair grows back. We'll be all right. Your hair will grow back. And again, I was assured.

After that, I don't even remember if we were given anything to dry ourselves with. I don't-- I don't think so. We were given these coarse striped uniforms, and wooden shoes, and made to go with all these other people. They led us to this barrack. That was-- as far as one could see, they were just all these wooden barracks. That was Birkenau that we were at.

And we were taken into there. And there were no bunks, nothing. We just sat on the floor and spent the night like that. And people were just talking amongst themselves. And I-- what is happening? Where-- where are we? We were supposed to go to this munition factory in Germany. We didn't stay there long.

Oh, the next morning though, I should tell you about a routine that was called that zahlappell. Early in the morning, and it was so cold, we were taken outside, and made to line up. And we were counted. That was a daily routine in-- in all the camps.

And we were given this metal bowl, in which we got the day's ration of soup, and a piece of dark bread. We were hungry at this point. We didn't eat I don't know how many days, in the train ride and all this. And we just ate it, just lapped it up and then realized that-- my mother did-- that we shouldn't eat it all at once. So we saved the bread to eat it a little later on.

And after a few days, the other women in this group, and my mother were try to find out why we were there instead of in the metal factory. And they asked somebody. And they said, indeed, we were supposed to have gone and that we will go. And so they were glad about that. I just remember seeing all these people, all these thin-looking people wearing the same striped, baggy things that we were wearing.

And it was muddy and dirty, and horrible, horrible. We didn't stay there long.

INT: How old were you at this time?

EU: 11, or 12. I'm trying to think but just about that time, 1944. I'm born in '32. I'm thinking out loud here. But I don't know.

INT: Do you remember where the barrack was, or the name or number of the barrack?

EU: No.

INT: Did you know at this time where your father was?

EU: No, I had no idea, no men at all, no idea. I was just-- there, like I said, a short time. And we were again taken on a train to be relocated, under similar conditions, the same kind of a situation, on a-- on a cattle car like that. And the women thought that we were going to the metal factory. Well, we arrived in another concentration camp called Stutthof. And I have horrible memories of Stutthof, the starvation, the lack of any-- of any bathroom facilities. And that was so horrible. That's so difficult to even talk about. But it was a very real thing.

It was a concentration camp just like Auschwitz, except smaller. As far as-- our daily routine was the same. And we were in this barrack in Stutthof, where one of the most awful memories of all the years happened to me. And I will tell you what that was.

We were sitting there on the floor. The barrack was divided into three equal parts lengthwise. There were people on either side, women. And in the middle was the so-called Lagerstrasse, where the guards would walk up and down. There were bunks, but I remember sitting, just bunks like shelves, really. I remember sitting on the floor with other women, with my mother and my grandmother sat across the Lagerstrasse with some other women.

And some young officers came in. I always remember the boots and the uniforms. I don't remember the faces. And they asked for some volunteers to do their clothes, sock mending. And my grandmother, who was a home economics teacher and very handy with a sewing needle, raised her hand immediately.

To this day, I remember my mother's grip on my hand. She wanted to shout-- she later told me-- to her mother, don't. But she couldn't, because that would have brought attention to me. And I was not to be seen. I was to keep a low profile as much as possible.

As the men gathered the women who volunteered, they took them outside. And one of them came back, turned back, laughing-- laughing. There was no mending to be done. They took the women outside and killed them.

There is no doubt in my mind that the promise of an extra piece of bread was the reason that my grandmother volunteered. And there is no doubt that she intended to give that bread to me. And when I think about her, which I do often, I try not to think of her horrible death, but about the love that she had for me. [PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS]

Life went on with the morning zahlappells, and the beatings, and the fear. There was one other girl somewhere in that camp, in that barrack, besides me. And I remember seeing her from a distance, just sort of the back of her head. I didn't see any other children.

Sometime while I was there, one morning, I shared the bunk with my mother. I couldn't move. I couldn't get up. My legs just gave out. There was an infirmary. But to bring that to anyone's attention, I think my mother realized at that time would not have been safe.

Amongst the prisoners, women prisoners that came with us, was a friend of hers who was a physician. And I remember her name, Dr. Zosha Kagen. Mother confided in her. She had no medication, nothing really to examine me with. But she felt that what I had was a paralysis that was common in cases of typhus that people developed.

And Dr. Zosha and Mother devised a plan, where during zahlappell, we would be crowded amongst other people. People stood close by anyway, because it was so cold. They to keep warm. We would be inside. And I would be sort of hanging on their arms, which were under my armpits, make it look like I was standing. And they pulled it off. Indeed, without any medication or any care, I did recover. I regained the strength in my legs.

Speaking of recovering or the strength that I had, I believe that one of the reasons that when we arrived at Auschwitz I wasn't immediately taken away with the other children, even though I was small, was the fact that because of the extra food that I had in the ghetto, I looked fairly healthy and maybe considered to be of labor force value.

But here we were, in-- in this horrible camp, horrible situations, when another remarkable episode happened. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] There was-- we knew there was a women's camp where we were. I say, we, the women knew, and that there was a men's camp also on the other side. We were totally separated. There were guards, by the way, in-- in the camp that were looking, overlooking us all the time. There was the barbed wire that was electrically charged, and there were guards constantly looking out. I was never to be seen. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

Being a child, a Jewish child during those times, we were condemned to death. And I knew that. I knew that. I knew to-- I trusted my mother completely. I knew to-- she would say [NON-ENGLISH], disappear. Just, I shouldn't be noticed, wherever possible. Get under something, behind people. So I was always aware of that. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

But at that time, or sometime around this time, in Stutthof, as a matter of fact, I do know the time. Because it was around October 29, 1944, [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] when a man, a nun, a Polish Catholic, I believe Polish man, an electrician worked on both sides of the camps. And people tried to give messages to him to take back and forth. Everyone was looking for their husbands, their fathers, their brothers. And somehow he did give Mother-- that he told her that, indeed, my father was in that camp also. He was ill. He was in bad condition. But he was there.

And I remember mother telling me this. And we were so happy. And this man said that for my birthday, my father would have a gift for me. He would have a spoon, a wooden spoon. Somehow, people were very resourceful and were trying to do whatever they could for the extra piece of bread. And someone had some pieces of wood on the other side, in the men's section, and was carving these spoons. I am sure if found out, that person would have been shot.

But anyway, for I don't know how much of his food ration-- he did obtain one of those spoons, and through this man, told Mother when he would throw the spoon over the fence on my birthday. And indeed, he did. I didn't know about it then. And my mother ran towards the spoon that he threw. They were appointed time. And the guard from the tower shot at her, missing her. But they knew that there was a woman running towards the fence.

The only thing they knew from up there, is that she wore a scarf. Mother somehow got a scarf for herself, and for me, a headscarf. And excuse me-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] the older-- they were looking for a woman in a scarf. Mother and a few of the other women who had scarves all took their scarves out, whatever they did with it, and she was never found out.

INT: Did she manage to pick up the spoon?

EU: No. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] That was probably one of the most loving gifts that I almost got. But we knew my father was there. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] As-- as the situation worsened for us still, became more and more dangerous, in Stutthof as a child, Mother tried to make herself useful. To be useful and resourceful meant survival, extra piece of bread. I cannot tell you how exciting it was to find a real potato in our daily soup, or a piece of one. The soup was made of potato peels and turnips.

And every time we did get, we talked about this. We just talked about that. Now, I didn't have a spoon, and we were just lapping it up like animals. But for the person dishing out the soup, to go deep down into the can and bring out was a real favor. It was really special. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

The women sitting around often exchanged recipes about what they cooked before the war. Food was all that anyone talked about that I remember. It was just hunger cannot be explained. So I won't even try to do that. But mother made herself more useful in Stutthof. And I think that again gave us extra, a little extra food. Eventually--

INT: How did she do that?

EU: She organized, first I remember, some kind of a routine with the latrines. And that way one of the kapos liked that, that someone was taking-- because it was pretty chaotic. And they were-- we were just beaten up. There were rules to follow. But they were not in any way-- no one on the inside-- amongst the Jewish women, you know, was in charge. So Mother in a way became in charge of our little group, and even at one time, got a-- some sort of a piece of fabric that hung like a curtain over our bunk, which allowed for more privacy. That was quite a luxury. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

After a while, we were to be relocated again. This time, it's just Mother and myself. And we were taken on a train. It was a cattle car train, but not as crowded as the other.

And this time we indeed ended up in a metal factory in the city of Dresden, in Germany. We were so lucky, so fortunate. We were still part of concentration camp, Flossenburg, and guarded by SS men. But now we were useful, which meant in order for us to work, to be-- there was a little more food. And we had bunks to sleep on.

And we worked. Mother and I always together, we worked shifts. She was working some kind of a machine, punching holes in metal. And I was working at a conveyor belt, sorting bullets. The factory was in the upper stories. And we stayed in the basement at night.

And so fate had me on this February night 1944, locked in a munition factory in Dresden, which was one of the worst bombed cities during the war. On and off, there would be sirens blaring, and it never materialized. You could hear bombs exploding away. But this was horrendous. Our building, with all of the other buildings, was hit. We were so fortunate that it was not an explosive, but an incendiary bomb.

The building, everything went up in smoke. We were made-- we worked that shift, that night shift. We were made to run to the basement. And I recall, there were sheets of metal on the floor stacked high, and the wind that was created by this firestorm, lifted them and brought them down in a deafening sound. It was an awful sound. There was total chaos. But we were still guarded. In the middle of all this, they still guarded us.

When the building was just totally burning, there a-- there was a-- some kind of a barrel with water. And mother grabbed a cloth, made it wet, and put it around my face, just so my eyes could see. And we were herded out of that building. A few strong young people managed to escape. I don't know where or who. Most of us were made to run along these burning streets.

And it was a horrendous sight. And I know that I was scared. I was so scared. I remember praying, saying the Shema. And at that time, people were, as afraid as they were, they thought this was the end. I'm sure everyone did, except my mother.

And they were at the same time glad. This was the first sign that someone was fighting the Germans. If we were to die, at least they were suffering too. So there was this mixed feeling of total terror and being grateful that someone out there maybe knew about us, and was trying to help.

We were taken outside this burning city, and spent the night on a meadow outside, in some fields, looking at the city just on fire. Spend the night there. The next morning we walked back to what was left of the factory, and stayed there. I don't remember how long. Stayed there, they I suppose didn't know what to do with us. And the way I understand at that time, there was-- there were no trains, even cattle cars, available for us.

INT: Did you spend any time in the camp of Flossenburg?

EU: No. No, we were only on their papers. And I remember it was we were on the papers of Flossenburg. The factory owners must have bought us, purchased us from the Flossenburg, and the Flossenburg SS guards were-- were guarding us. But-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] that is all I remember.

I also remember, now that just came to me, that one day when we came back to our bunks, on Mother's and mine which we always shared, was under this blanket or whatever that was, a piece of bread. And it had to be somebody, some-- some German guard, I suppose, who left it there.

INT: We'll stop now and change the tape.INT: Your stay in Dresden after the bombing. Please continue.

EU: From there, we walked. They walked us. We didn't know where to, along the river Elbe in Germany. And it was cold. And we were weak, and tired, and sick, and so undernourished. We walked all day. Those people who could no longer walk were just left along the way or shot. The nights we spent usually in open soccer stadiums, where we would sit and pluck lice from each other's hair, crush the lice with our fingernails. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

One night we spent in a barn and some hay. And some SS guard, the women, came through with pitchforks in the night and stabbed wherever. And I remember people screaming and shrieking. And I don't know how many of them came out to keep going the next day. We did.

INT: Why were they doing that?

EU: Form of entertainment, I suppose-- just sheer brutality. Women were doing that. I also remember when a nail embedded itself-- a nail in my wooden shoe-- embedded itself in my left foot. And it was so painful, I could barely walk.

And Mother asked a guard if we could stop where we could somehow try and get that nail out. And she said no. And I just kept hopping. How I didn't get blood infection at that point, I don't know. I still have a scar on that spot. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

I remember going through little towns, picturesque, beautiful little German towns. And I remember that one place in particular where we came, it was dusk, and came through this town. Often, people would just look at us, seeing this group. I don't know how many we were, all these people walking.

And I-- looking up at the windows, I remember these homes with pretty curtains, and behind them, light windows. And there were people living with their families, and-- and food, and beds. And I thought, oh. It was like a fairy tale. I don't remember what I really-- if I ever thought this would be again possible for me. But I remember thinking, isn't that beautiful that they-- they have that sort of life? [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

We also found out, somewhere along the way, that my father was with the men's group also. And at one point, one stadium at night, Mother managed to get a piece of bread. She gave it to me. And I was to relay it to get somehow to someone on the other side where the men were. And I remember going down there in between people and handing it to someone, hoping that he would get it. It was a horrible, horrible time.

We were near our strength. And what my mother found out, so was my father. There was time that he just barely could make it. But we kept going. And it was just, again, making it through that day. That was, when I look back on it, our resistance, to-- to live, to go on.

I must tell you one other thing now, while we're doing this interview. No matter where I was, there were always people saying, whoever survives must tell our story-- must tell our story, whoever it is. The world should know. Because we then felt that no one knew about us. Surely, if they did, they would come to our rescue. Whoever survives has to tell. And I feel that a very strong obligation [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] to do. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

As we walked, which I now know it was 12 days, eventually, we reached another camp, which was Theresienstadt. And I remember entering that camp and thinking, this is unlike any concentration camp I've ever seen. It was a prison.

But there were-- we were led into the barracks, or the rooms, and there were tables. And there were bunks. And it was so different from anything that I-- that I have seen before.

And we were there a short time, when we could hear shots. And rather-- very suddenly-- very suddenly, the guards were gone. And Russian soldiers in their tanks entered the camps. This all happened so quickly.

INT: Where were you when this happened?

EU: In Theresienstadt.

INT: Where-- where in Theresienstadt?

EU: In a building. I don't know which one, but I remember looking out a window, looking out, and seeing what was happening. These soldiers were coming with the tanks. And there were no guards, no Germans left.

And the feeling that, indeed, maybe this was over was overwhelming-- overwhelming. People were hugging, and crying, and laughing. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And it was-- I-- I-- I have a difficult time describing the joy and, at the same time, the pain that others felt, and the bewilderment. We didn't know what was happening. But we knew that it was over in a way.

The first thing that the Russians did was give us candy-- food. I remember eating, and eating, and eating all these candies that they-- the-- the first group, they gave us, getting sick, vomiting, and eating some more.

And in the midst of all this chaos, people were running around, looking for others, maybe anyone. And while my mother's first thought was finding my father, indeed, we did. He too was in Theresienstadt. And I remember when we first met, and hugged, and cried. And he looked so weak and terrible. But we were alive. It was [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] an overwhelming feeling of both joy and loss. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

At that point, after a few days-- some people, by the way left immediately. We didn't leave the first day or two. But then-- and right away, people started making plans. Now what? And many people were heading towards Prague. And we, too, got a ride with some others towards the city of Prague. We were still consumed by just the need to eat more. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

We arrived in Prague. And that city opened its hearts to us. I remember so well. There were everywhere people that arranged Red Cross or other organizations, shelters, hospitals, schools, hotels opened their doors to us.

And we were taken into a hotel, into a hotel room, where we were examined by doctors. My foot was bandaged. My father was very ill. He was told by the doctor, he must stay in bed and whatever medication was given. And I remember taking my first bath, and they gave us some clothes, putting those on, and sitting at a table, and eating. And that was so extraordinary. But most of all, the three of us were together. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

My parents, at that point, talking with other people, were thinking originally of going back to Lódz. But already, rumors were coming back from some people-- I don't know how long, by the way, we were in Prague-- who went back home, only to find out that they were not welcome.

Antisemitism was still very much prevalent in-- in Poland. Many of the people who returned, the survivors, were told to leave again. People didn't want to part with their belongings, which they have acquired from people who were taken away.

And the thought occurred to my parents that if anybody-- and surely, they had hoped, some would be-- any relatives that survived, they would all want to reach my famous uncle, Leon Goldman, in Switzerland.

This was my Grandmother Wolman's brother, who, according to family legend, was really quite an unusual person. He was one of the first fliers to fly with Sikorsky. As a young man, he left Poland and went to Switzerland. He was also one of the founders of Swiss Air, we learned later. Anyway, if anybody was left from the family, they would be in touch with Uncle Leon.

INT: Had you ever met Uncle Leon?

EU: I had never met him. But I heard stories about him. And my father-- of course, that was his-- this was my second uncle. He-- he knew about Uncle Leon. And anyone in the family knew that. Also, at that point, there were some rumors that the Russians were already closing the borders.

Anyway, there we were, in the hotel room-- I didn't know all of this, what was happening-- when suddenly, one day, two young men appeared at the door. I remember, one of them had red hair. They pushed their way in, confronted my father, saying that they wanted all the gold, and jewelry, all the things that he had from the ghetto, to give to them.

And my father-- we had nothing. We had absolutely nothing. Well, they said, they would be back within an hour. And if my parents didn't turn over all these things that they demanded, they would take me.

And I remember how I felt then. I was so terribly scared. This was against me, personally. Here we were, free, finally together. My parents were still traumatized, really, not knowing what to do. There was no one to turn to.

We didn't know what to do. My mother tried to reason. They are bluffing. There is nothing. We have nothing. We never had anything. If they wanted to take me, they probably would do it. So right away, we didn't know what was going on. But they decided that we better leave.

And we left the hotel. We had nothing-- no luggage, nothing. And we went to the train station in Prague. The train stations were where all-- everything was happening, where people were registering, where food was distributed.

We walked to the train station. And there were lots of other people, some looking for relatives, for-- others, just wandering, bewildered. It was a very busy, busy place. And we were just kind of looking as to what now, when my parents met an acquaintance, someone, and told him-- told him what was happening about this-- this-- this encounter.

And that man then told them that that wasn't that unusual, that they were some people, some young people who, besides looking for revenge against the Germans, were also looking for revenge against anyone they perceived as having been in any authority in the camps or in the ghettos.

And this man told my parents that only a few days before, someone that they both knew, I don't know who, was severely beaten, and that there was no one to turn to. There was no authority. It was the law of the street.

And that man then suggested that maybe it would be a good idea for my parents, for time being, to change their name. And here comes this episode that I find so very difficult to relay, because under normal conditions, it sounds absolutely ridiculous.

But in the moment, the-- the way they were then ill, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] traumatized, scared, it just seemed like, yes, whatever-- whatever is necessary. This man, somehow, produced a paper, which I am not quite sure if it was his and not collected, as I assumed, or some other way, in which there were names.

And my father, my mother, and I assumed the name of Krieger. Suddenly, then and there, I was told that my name was no longer Wolman. I was Eva Krieger. My father was David Krieger. My mother was [? Erna ?] Krieger. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

It was an-- one of the most traumatic memories of my entire life, just having been suddenly transformed into someone else. I didn't realize it then. I really didn't. But looking back, I think about that 12-year-old Krysia. [PAUSES FOR 7 SECONDS]

After wandering around the train station and eating some hard-boiled eggs that were distributed, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] it was getting late. And we-- there was a train standing. And there were no trains moving. They-- you couldn't buy a ticket to go somewhere. There were just some trains moving, some trains standing.

We got in on that train and sat down. We were the only people on the train, at least in that car that I know of. There was no one else. And I just dozed off. And eventually, the train started moving. And we didn't know where it was going to. We had no idea. We just sat in it.

Well, the train stopped. And we got off and went on another train, which was, again, leaving. And that one ended up in the city of Dresden. Here we were, in Dresden-- again, the train station filled with people from all over the world. People were just always trying to find-- to go somewhere or come back from something.

INT: With the new identity of Krieger--

EU: Yes.

INT: --what nationality were you?

EU: I was German nationality. And I don't quite know the very specifics, but in order to continue towards Switzerland, to continue west, which was their aim to do at this point, toward Uncle Leon, we did not want to have-- they did, I should say, because I had no say about this. Polish nationality could have been maybe seen as being, at one point, sent back to Poland.

This would have allowed us easier passage through Germany towards Switzerland. So that's an important point. Because up till that time, I spoke nothing but Polish.

And speaking of that, if I may, I would like to mention that when I was a little girl, before the war, in Lódz, my family spoke nothing but Polish. Polish was our-- was-- was my language. Both my parents knew German-- maybe not fluently at first, but they studied it. And of course, I didn't. I just knew Polish.

And here I was, Eva Krieger, Jewish, coming from a concentration camp. But German is my nationality. And I didn't speak the language. And I remember feeling-- saying, what am I to do? And I was told, just don't talk. And having before followed orders and always been safe, there was no doubt in my mind. I could do that. I've done more difficult things than not to speak.

When we arrived in this town, they found out-- there were other people too-- that there close by was the border between the Russian occupation of Germany and the Allied, meaning English, French, and American troops.

INT: This is after you left Dresden?

EU: Yes. Yes. And we were there. It was a small town, somewhere on-- I don't know how far from Dresden. But that's where the border was. And there were not many, but there were some other people my parents talked to with the-- they were trying to get on to the Allied zone.

And we walked through some fields. We had nothing to carry-- walked and walked with some of the other people, looking around, and came around the other side. And we were in the Allied zone, which was very much where everyone wanted to be. As it turned out, that was the French occupation force. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

We had nothing to eat again. And it never occurred to my parents that, as survivors, they could have gone into any German place and taken anything at that moment in time that they wanted. This was never an option, never even talked about.

What was customary then, what was done, and they were aware of it, is when one came to a German town, to go to city hall The city hall was the place where the occupation forces removed the Nazi mayors and other people working there and installed their own. And it was at city hall that people would get some help with a place to stay, with some money, and with some food until they went on to the next place.

There was all this, everyone moving around. And that's what we did, going west, whatever was moving, if it was some train, or if it was a truck. We landed-- I remember one place clearly.

We landed in Heidelberg, a beautiful little city close to the city of Mannheim that was not bombed. Usually, the pattern was there would be a large city bombed out and a city close by that wasn't bombed. And I remember sitting there, along the river, on a bench, and overlooking this lovely spot.

And we were-- we went to the city hall. We had a place to spend the night. I don't remember particularly where. And while we were sitting there on the bench, someone came and gave us some sandwiches. And I remember sitting there. We were so grateful for any help like that.

From Heidelberg, we were-- we got a ride to the city of Heilbronn. And from there, the aim was Stuttgart-- Stuttgart, southern Germany, getting closer to the Swiss border. And we rode on a truck. Someone gave us the ride. We still didn't have anything, really, to carry with us-- maybe some-- an extra sweater or something.

And as we were approaching Stuttgart, the driver stopped. And he said, Stuttgart is bombed out completely. You won't be able to find a place for overnight. Why don't you stay in this smaller town 14 kilometers away called Ludwigsburg? And then the next day, you can go on further.

And I remember so clearly being dropped off in front of this big church, Garnisonskirche, on the steps, tall steps leading up to it. That was in the Stuttgarter Strasse, in the town of Ludwigsburg, which I never heard about.

And as we got off the truck, my father, up on the staircases, by the church, grabbed his chest and couldn't breathe, couldn't talk. And we got him sat down. And I was so scared. And he was just in such pain. And Mother said, stay with him. And she ran for help.

And somehow, she came back a little while later with some man. And my father was regaining his consciousness. And with this man's help, we made it, after waiting a while, to the city hall in Ludwigsburg [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] and there met the mayor. We were the only survivors to come to this town.

And this man. His name was Herr Beringer. He was French, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] appointed to this post by the French occupiers, immediately befriended us. It was so remarkable that this man, just without asking too many questions, saw these three people in such need of help, and was there for us.

He suggested that we spend the night not in a hotel of some sort, but he knew of an apartment where some Nazis fled, short while before-- days, weeks, I don't know. And that was the street-- the address was Anlagenstrasse 13 in Ludwigsburg. He escorted us there, brought us some food, and we spent that first night there.

The next day, a doctor examined my father. Herr Beringer sent the doctor to us. And my father apparently suffered a heart attack. And the man-- and the doctor said that he must rest. He must regain his strength somehow, prescribed medications.

And I remember being in that apartment. My parents were uneasy-- my father, especially. Mother, right away, started making arrangements. But I remember looking at it and admiring everything. That's the first time that we were in what seemed like a home.

And we stayed there for a while with the help of Mr. Beringer. And when my dad regained his strength to a point, my parents decided that, as peaceful and nice as this was, there was no future for us to live there.

Also, the awareness that we were living under this false identity was suddenly beginning, really, to sink in. What do we do about this? Now, here, this man, our papers say Krieger. He knows us as Krieger. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Didn't know, really, how to-- what to do.

They couldn't see any way out, except to leave, and somehow, managed to get information about some people from a nearby DP camp-- Displaced Persons camp-- who were supposed to go to Palestine. And somehow, after a long decision and-- decided that that is what they would do. My mother, after all, had two brothers in Palestine and that that would be the future for us. They did not want to stay in Germany. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

After saying goodbye to Mr. Beringer, we joined that transport. I remember thinking, oh, in a way, I wanted to stay. But I had no say about this. And they were convinced they were doing the right thing. We joined up with these trucks that were I don't know how many. But there were other survivors who were led by Israelis, taking these people, us included, what we thought was going to be to Palestine.

And as we traveled on this truck, talking a little bit to people-- it was pretty silent group, actually-- at one point, at the stop, the truck stop, and the driver got off, Mother struck up a conversation with him, with this Israeli driver, and told him about her brother, Eleizer Kafeman, in Israel.

And just so happened, this man, this young driver, knew my uncle. He knew my uncle, Eleizer Kafeman, in Israel, and told my mother that she should know, we were not heading straight to Palestine. We were going, along with all the other transports, to Cyprus.

INT: We need to stop now and change the tape.INT: Father on the convoy, on your way to Palestine.

EU: Yes, they very much wanted to go to Palestine, but when these young Israeli soldiers told my parents what conditions would be like where we were actually going, to Cyprus, and he felt obligated, he said, to tell this to Eliezer Kafeman's sister, because we would be in a camp. And no telling how long we would be there.

And he said, the conditions weren't the best. They were crowded, people-- after all, nobody wanted us, the remnants of Holocaust survivors. And there was no other place to go, at that time, except to go through this procedure. And he, in a way, discouraged my parents. And realizing their physical and mental condition, they were as much in favor as they were of the existence of a Jewish state, they were-- they felt in no condition to be in another camp for any prolonged time.

As it turned out, we were outside Munich. Well, we never got back on the truck. The truck drove away, and there we were, the three of us, alone. Managed to get back to Munich. And with no other option that they could see, after searching their mind and heart, returned to Ludwigsburg and Mr. Beringer.

INT: Had you been able to contact your uncle in Switzerland at this time?

EU: Not at that point. Not yet. And I think that thinking about this, I think it was-- it was just such a difficult time with this name situation. I think it had something to do with this, but I am not positive how it was. But we returned back to Ludwigsburg, and back to the Anlagenstrasse apartment.

Mr. Beringer at that time had a plan, after we were there for a few days. And my father, by the way, was better, but not well by any means.

This gentleman thought that with his connections as mayor of Ludwigsburg, and with my mother's energy-- that he detected right away-- it would be a good idea for them to start doing some sort of something to support themselves. And his idea was that they could open up a shop, souvenir shop, for the Allied troops only.

And by the way, at this point, the Americans were in charge of that area, not the French. And a souvenir shop, at which he would help with getting the merchandise-- because there was nothing available, no stores were open, by the way. And my mother would-- my parents and I would run the little store.

Just like he found the Anlagenstrasse apartment, he found a little store in the-- on the main street in Ludwigsburg-- Kirchstrasse-- this was all to be for time being. Named the store after his daughter, Valeria. And now, it became our daily routine to look for merchandise that we could sell.

The store was open from 12:00 till 2:00 every day. And we would make things-- everything possible, we would sit at night in the dining room by the table, and Mother would, again, make her pretty felt things. I would write 'Souvenir of Germany' on anything-- empty boxes, pencils. And my father was in charge of the book department-- of keeping the books.

The word got around that there was this little shop for the American soldiers. And often, when we came to open it, they would be lined up, sitting outside of the pavement-- Americans loved souvenirs. And that was the only little store opened in Ludwigsburg.

It was about that time, too, that my parents felt, since-- since we're staying there-- for now, at least, it was never to be a permanent situation-- that I need some schooling. And they inquired-- no one really knew quite what to do with me. I was 12 years old. But I never had more school than just a little bit in the ghetto.

I spoke at that point a little German that I picked up. And they decided to put me with my age group. Well, it was a rather difficult situation. I had private-- I went to school with these kids. I no longer-- I no more belonged in there. I must have just looked like a-- just really stood out like a sore thumb.

Also, I should mention that my hair was growing in slowly. I already had a little hair then, but I thought I looked like a boy. So I managed to find pieces of fabric and make ribbons out of them, and tie them into my hair and around my neck. And I enjoyed that the little apartment so much-- the bathroom with the mirror-- and I just was beginning to kind of get to-- to feel like a girl again.

And so I-- I started at school. I had a German, English, and French as mandatory. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] German and English were never really quite a problem-- French, I found, even though the most beautiful language, I found it difficult. But every day after school, I would walk down to this teacher and have-- have lessons all along.

INT: What type of lessons?

EU: Lessons in the languages, and arithmetic, and all the subjects, which I never took quite seriously in those days. This was all so trivial to me. But I always enjoyed painting and drawing. I spent my evenings, every free moment I had, doing that.

I also enjoyed reading. And I was frustrated that I didn't have enough guidance as to really what to read. So here we were--

INT: Did you read in German?

EU: Yes, yes. I can't tell you when I-- German came easy, for some reason. I never-- German grammar is supposed to be difficult, I never found that. And English was not too terribly difficult, either.

INT: Do you remember the name of the school you went to?

EU: Yes, it was good [SPEAKING GERMAN]-- it was a girls' school. And I know that people must have known-- they knew-- they knew that we were in concentration camps. But no one asked, and we didn't talk. We didn't talk about our past. We didn't even talk amongst ourselves.

Which, looking back, I find mind boggling. But we didn't. It was all now-- how-- what to do? How to go about? Mother, the strong, incredibly positive, creative woman had a new challenge. My father wasn't feeling well. She had a business to run.

Life took on the daily importance of the daily life, of seeing to it-- we had to have income. And so this is what she did. And I-- that went on for a while.

And then, miracle of miracles, who would have ever thought? My brother was born. I had a little brother who was born in 1947.

Those two years seem to have gone by so quickly. But here was my little brother, whose name is Eli, born after the war, named for my grandfather. And I remember then telling my mother how much, when I was a child, I had wished I had a brother or a sister. And she-- I remember her answer, because she said, in German-- at that point, we spoke German-- she said, [SPEAKING GERMAN]-- I should have had two, yet.

That was her only admission ever that it must have been difficult to see me through. She never complained. That was all past. She knew we would live. And we were alive, and she had another child, and life was going on.

And my name, by the way, I was called Eva, which was changed from-- well, it was-- that was what I was called, not Krysia. I'm regressing here for a minute. But it was about that time that I think my parents felt secure enough, and the situation were stable enough, that they realized they wanted their identity back.

By the way, Mr. Beringer, about that time, too, left back for France. My parents paid him whatever business transaction there was, and the store was now theirs. It also was open to everybody. And competition set in, and mother had a real task on her hands to-- to see to it that the business was going all right, because it was-- we no longer had Mr. Beringer's protection.

INT: Let me ask you, was there a Jewish community at all in Ludwigsburg?

EU: There wasn't in Ludwigsburg. There was one other woman that we knew that was Jewish who was in hiding during the war. And we did meet her and her children.

There were some people in Stuttgart. And it was sort of the start of the Jewish community. And my parents were-- in a way, they were connected with it, but not actively participating. Stuttgart was 14 kilometers away, and that was quite a distance by train. And they just didn't-- did not have the time or the inclination.

But it was-- we were part-- on the list. Passover coming around, we picked up matzah, we got various information in the mail. Anything dealing with the existence of the state of Israel was very important to my father.

I want to mention one other thing, too, that they-- the store where we lived was-- was a long walk. And I'm mentioning it because those are some of my fondest memories of my dad and me. My parents would leave the lights in the windows-- the display windows-- after the store closed, on.

And every evening, 9 o'clock, some-- something like that, when it was dark, come rain, snow, didn't matter, my father and I would walk down there and-- to turn off the lights, and walk back. And we discussed everything.

My father was-- had quite a knowledge of history and politics, and we would talk about everything, he and I, never talking about our experience. It just wasn't talked about. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] It--

INT: Let me ask you, when you were living under the name Krieger, as a German--

EU: Yes.

INT: --your were also Jewish?

EU: Absolutely.

INT: That was not concealed.

EU: We were-- that was never-- oh, no, no, we were very Jewish. When my brother was born, he had a bris. A man from Stuttgart came to perform that. And our Jewish identity was not Orthodox in-- by any standards, but very much aware of it. This was a nationality that they had hoped to keep for a time, for a short time. Our Jewishness was never, never changed at any way.

INT: Did you have friends from school, or other places, during this time?

EU: Well, I sort of did, but I don't think it was real friendship, except for one friend who is a friend to this very day, that stayed a friend of mine. I just must not have fitted in.

INT: What is her name?

EU: Her name is Gerda Dachtler, and we may not see each other in 10 years, but when we're together, it's as if we had talked half an hour ago. She was my good friend. And I didn't talk with her for a long time, either, but eventually, I did a little bit, the only one that I did.

At that point, after my brother was born, and Mother had the business to run, a lady came to live with us to take care of my brother. She came from Sudetenland, a German woman, she was Catholic. She came to stay and take care of my brother.

She wasn't married. She had no family. She became my adopted grandmother. Who would have ever thought, then, that she would stay with my family till she died 20 some years later? My mother cared for her, if she had her own mother.

INT: What was her name?

EU: And I was very fond of her. Her name was Irmgard Fuhrach.

Yet also, I must mention another couple in those days. Any time my parents met someone Jewish, it was like meeting a relative, immediate family, anybody. We were so isolated that way.

And they met this couple, a German-- a Jewish American captain and his wife, Bill and Ieda Kay, and they became good friends. It was the Kays who introduced me to my first taste of anything American, and I loved it. I don't know how old I was then, probably 14 or so, but I remember my first Seventeenmagazine. I remember my first Coke and how wonderful all that was.

They gave me jeans, and showed me how American girls roll them up and wore them with white socks and penny loafers. It was such a-- and all the things they told me about the country, and I would just look at the magazines and see it, and just a such carefree, wonderful life-- I was totally infatuated with it all.

And I remember thinking when I was walking down the street in Ludwigsburg, I wanted everyone to think that I was an American girl. I dressed like it and I copied their makeup, and all that sort of thing.

So at that point, the important part was for my parents, they wanted their identity back. And they hired an attorney, and told him-- a German attorney-- their entire story, what has happened, and who they really were.

And it was a long process, a long process. But it was such a wonderful feeling, just having started this attempt, it was at that time that they did get in touch with Uncle Leon in Lausanne, who indeed came to see us. And we prepared for him as if royalty were expected-- a relative, a relative. It was wonderful.

Also getting in touch with my mother's brothers-- both her brothers, and uncle Leon, were looking for us in Europe. But there was no trace of the Wolman family. So they couldn't find us.

My mother's brothers had to write affidavits telling that they knew who we were. And other people, also, to vouch for our true identity. Through my uncle in Israel, he found Dr. Zosha from Stutthof, who lived in Israel, who was very ill at that time. But of course, wrote and said, yes, I know who they are.

INT: Was she living in Israel at that time?

EU: She was in Israel. It was a long, drawn-out process. And in the meantime, life went on. One had to survive. And in the midst of all this, there were happy times, too. Those were my teenage years. And I-- I remember liking German music.

I remember in the evening, we would sit around the big dining room table. I would usually draw, paint. Mother would knit or embroider. My father was reading. Those were happy times, and even though, under strange conditions.

My-- my parents befriended other people. It was still, no one talked, no one asked.

INT: Do you remember the name of the German attorney that helped your family?

EU: No, I don't. I don't at all. I don't think I even ever met him. My studies were on and off, going OK. And-- and it was 1950 by time-- by the time that my parents finally-- and whatever it took-- finally we finally got our names back.

My father was again [? Marek ?] Wolman, mother, Esther Wolman, and I kept Eva as my first name, because I was used to it. And because that's what I was known to at school. And Krysia was someone way back there.

INT: What effect, living under that different name, for those five years, do you think that had on you?

EU: I think it had a tremendous effect on me. It did on all of us. But my mother is such a fighter, and she had to worry about now. And she-- to her, it was just going on with life, never really looking back. My father brooded about it a lot.

And I think, to me, it was absolutely horrendous. It left-- it left me looking over my shoulder for years and years to come. I'm still uncomfortable with it. It has left very painful, very painful. And I am just so glad that they finally got to correct that and claim their true identity back. Excuse me.

INT: What about your nanny from Lódz? Did you ever hear from her, or about her after the war?

EU: No, we never did. We were never in touch with anyone from Lódz, ever. I have not been back, I have no-- I have read all I could, and still do. But we have not had any contact with anyone.

So here we were, 1950, I was getting on with my school. And it was about '51 or '52, I don't know, that I started taking lessons, art classes, in Stuttgart at the [SPEAKING GERMAN], and I truly loved it.

I must mention one other episode because it was so important. It was earlier, while I was probably 13 or 14, the very first time that a Jewish organization collected some survivor children in-- in Europe, different countries, or in Germany, that I know of, and sent us to Switzerland for some better-- to-- to fatten us up. We were-- I mean, I was a little skeleton, you know, too.

And I was there at this home for Jewish children, in this lovely place, in Appenzell called Heiden. It was a place that was run by Mr. and Mrs. Knoyfeld, wonderful people, where I experienced for the first time some very important and lovely traditions of my Jewish heritage.

I remember [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] the singing Sabbath songs, and how-- how lovely it all was, how I was looking forward to Friday nights. We-- it was geared towards the needs of children. We went hiking, and we had fun. I went back several times.

The last time I went back, helping with younger children, and I was-- the last time I went, I was reimbursed with some Swiss francs. And I recall, on my way back, stopping in Zurich and going on a shopping spree-- buying gifts for my parents, for myself. And I was incredibly fond of these people.

My parents, in this time, their Jewish experience was-- they always said Kaddish for their parents, lit a candle. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] We always observed Passover by not eating bread. We always fasted on Yom Kippur. But there was no real community living. There-- the joy that I experienced in Heiden, I did not have at home.

The lady who took care of my brother, I was very fond of. We walked, long walk, she was a-- she loved nature, and she pointed out to my little brother and to me, when I went along, every butterfly, and every tree, and every flower. And to this day, I like those things. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

As time was moving on, and in the store, we had, of course, American soldiers still as customers-- not the only ones. But one day, a couple GIs came in, and Mother struck up a conversation with potential customers. And one of them either showed her, or she noticed, a Star of David.

And as isolated as we were, like I mentioned before, any contact with anybody Jewish was just so important, and so-- something that we looked forward to. Well, this young man introduced himself to my parents, and Mother-- he would come every other day around closing time, he would show up at the store, in his uniform.

And I talked to him, but not at any great length. It was really my mother who always had a conversation going. Because at this point, not only did she-- did she speak German fluently, she thought she spoke English perfectly well. She didn't, but she spoke. And if someone didn't understand her in her way, that was their problem, not hers.

So she established a rapport with all those American GIs. And this young man kept coming back, and Mother invited him for a home cooked dinner. And afterwards, they would play gin rummy.

Well, little did I know that this was Herb Unterman, who was coming home with us that time, who eventually became my husband. And he enjoyed playing gin rummy with my mother. And then one day, he asked me if I would like to go to Stuttgart to the GI service club with him.

And I said, oh, yes, anything American, fine. I was dating, I was about 18 at that point. And it sounded wonderful. And of course, I went. I was so excited about it. And I had a wonderful time.

I had-- he was so carefree, and I had fun. And we kept going out together. At that point, when he came over, I just didn't-- I just wanted to go, you know. I was a teenager, I didn't want to stay at home and all this. And for a while, I think, Mother missed the-- the card games.

But she was happy that I was enjoying myself. And there was some talk about maybe getting married, maybe me coming to the States. But time was running out. And his turn of duty in Germany was coming to an end.

We only knew him-- my parents and I-- for a short time, relatively, a few months. And so, Herb left. And I remember being devastated.

But he left with the promise that he would write soon. And quite a while went by-- I don't remember how long, maybe because I was so anxious that I didn't hear from him. And then, indeed, I got a letter saying that he missed me and wanted me to come to the US, and would I marry him?

And of course, I was overjoyed. My parents had some misgivings-- leaving, strange country, far away. They never thought about themselves at that point, at all. But it was what would be best for me. In a way, they didn't want to stand in the way of my happiness, but they were concerned-- where-- who-- where I was going, what would happen to me?

And in my mind, there was no doubt. As our correspondence continued, I just was going to go to the US. And I was reading everything about the United States, and just imagining myself in this country.

And as preparation for marriage, I was so totally naive, and-- and very-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] didn't really deal with the reality as I probably should have. I was collecting little knick-knacks, you know, that I would have in my future home. And I was very idealistic, very idealistic, thinking everything will be wonderful. I wasn't thinking about when would I see my family again, as much as I love them. I was ready to take off and-- and have this new wonderful adventure, a new life ahead of me.

Looking into immigration to the United States, I found out that we were stateless, as survivors, all-- [INAUDIBLE]. When it came to immigration, because I was born in Poland, I would be on the Polish quota. And that meant years and years of waiting.

But I found out that the Canadian-- that-- that Canada was admitting more people. And I decided to visit the Canadian Embassy in Karlsruhe. And when I went there, I saw these posters on the wall where they were looking, Canada was looking for domestic help to come to Canada.

